I have a very disturbing story for you  today about guns and race and good guys  with guns that we have to talk about and  and there is this very racialized  pro-gun argument that is sometimes made  around people of color and guns  sometimes you hear this from from the  pro-gun right when they want to pretend  as though the NRA is not an  extraordinarily racially tinged  organization and what they say is this  is their argument not mine we want  everybody to have access to legal guns  including people of color and the reason  it's important for people of color to  have legal access to guns is that they  are more likely to live in dangerous  neighborhoods and sometimes they have  more of a need to defend themselves  against other people who are violent  that's sort of the argument that you  hear them make and yet we rarely hear  about the NRA coming to the public  defense of black people who had firearms  or used firearms for self-defense or  merely had guns on them when they had an  interaction with the police we've even  had some specific cases we've covered in  the last year and a half that I've told  you about where you had legal a gun  ownership by black Americans that got  them into trouble when they were stopped  by police and in many of those the NRA  literally said nothing that's the sort  of pretext the context cut to this  weekend in Illinois 26 year-old black  man Jamel Roberson was working security  at a bar called Manny's Blue Room in the  Chicago suburb of Robbins Illinois there  was a shooting at the bar and Jamel  robbed Roberson Roberson was there  working security like I said Roberson  was able to apprehend the shooter and  when police showed up they shot and  killed the black security guard Jamel  Roberson who had again apprehended the  actual shooter the police were called  and were responding to the officer who  killed Roberson is white and you be the  judge of whether that's a factor as we  talk about the story the way that it  went down  is that Roberson had asked some drunk  people at the bar to leave a common  occurrence at bars one of those drunk  people came back with a gun and started  shooting Roberson was able to get the  shooter down on the ground with his knee  on the shooters back to prevent him from  getting up and according to what I've  read had taken the shooters gun and was  holding it on the shooters back along  with his knee waiting for police to  arrive now there is one report that says  Roberson had his own gun as well as a  valid firearms ID card the other reports  say that it was the shooters gun that  Robertson Roberson had appropriated for  himself but either way when police  showed up he had the suspect down on the  ground knee on his back gun on his back  police show up and shoot the security  guard dead as people were reportedly  reportedly yelling he's security he's  security the police who are now  investigating say that when police  showed up they yelled at the security  guard dropped the gun dropped the gun  and eventually shot him dead they also  say that part of the confusion was that  Roberson wasn't wearing any clothing  that identified him or indicated that he  was working security although there is a  conflicting report which says he had a  hat that said security at the same time  a lot of people just wear hats that say  security or shirts that say security  that's become popular so where that  plays into this is not totally clear  right now a lawsuit has been filed  claiming that Roberson civil rights were  violated this now is going to extend for  a long time with this legal case and  also notable here and tragic is that the  actual shooter in the initial bar  shooting as of this morning hadn't yet  been charged so my question is where's  the nra on this Roberson had a valid  federal of firearms identification card  he did not have a concealed carry  license we heard from the Midlothian  Illinois police chief what we have  learned is Jamel Roberson was a brave  man who was doing his best to end an  active shooter situation at this bar so  what are we to make of this Pat because  the defenders of the police are saying  listen police  were called to an active-shooter  situation they show up and a man is  kneeling on another man's back with a  gun pointed at him any reasonable police  officer would say oh we were called to a  shooting we see a guy with a gun that's  the shooter let's do something about it  but is it actually that simple if the  officer had been black or the security  guard white would it have played out the  exact same way and I don't deny for a  second Pat and you come from a law  enforcement family Pat there's no  question that a quick judgment has to be  made by police in these situations and  the issue here is whether that quick  judgment was in any way influenced by  race and I don't know whether we will  ever actually have that answer well  certainly the NRA doesn't want to cross  paths with law enforcement because they  do make up its base of supporters but I  mean there are many examples this is  just the latest one two years ago  falando Casteel a black man driving in  minnesota was pulled over by a white  police officer and he did the right  thing falando Castille did he said that  I have a gun on me I just want to make  sure that you know and then he reached  for I don't know if it was his phone or  his license and registration he reached  for something and the cop said don't  reach for it don't reach for it and shot  him dead  so his civil rights were also violated  no due process there he followed the  formula for what you're supposed to do  if you're pulled over and you have a gun  on you and yet he was shot dead and the  nra stayed silent on that one too  I want to hear from you about this what  do you make of it we are going to be  posting some comments that we receive on  this story on the david pakman show  instagram page at david pakman show  while you're there you can follow me at  David Pakman we will take a quick break  and be back after this with our  investigation into what is the solution  to the social media free speech D  platforming problem that many say we  have that's next
 

